 negative controls (single episodes); passes both negative controls\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claim[12] cites only the control_* metric deltas; source_evidence/evaluate_final.py lines ~1241-1255 and ~1272 (controls_ok is completeness/validity only, with no performance threshold); paper_data.json final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.candidate_diagnostics_trust=unverified; paper.tex Experimental Protocol (Controls and qualification).\n  - requested_fix: Reword to \"predictions degrade as expected under both negative controls (single episodes: permuted MAE +0.156, Spearman -1.157; constant MAE +0.094)\". Drop \"passes\", or state explicitly that no pass/fail criterion for the controls is evidenced.\n38:Evaluating a language model on every item of a benchmark is expensive, so a natural shortcut is to run the model on a small item subset and extrapolate its full-benchmark accuracy, using the per-item results of previously evaluated ``source'' models to choose the subset. We study this setting with binary per-item results, a budget of $k{=}100$ items, and two kinds of new model: targets that resemble the sources and targets stronger than every source. We evaluate a configuration that fits a logistic latent-ability model to the source matrix, picks items by greedy Fisher D-optimal design at upper-tail abilities, estimates target ability from the $k$ answers, and combines a calibrated subset mean with latent-model estimates through non-negative stacking weights fitted only on source pseudo-targets. On five held-out benchmarks (three seeds), it attains a mean absolute error (MAE) of 0.0294 against 0.0339 for a random-subset mean, 0.0428 for $k$-means anchor points and 0.0692 for an IRT-style estimator; it also passes both negative controls (single episodes). However, the paired bootstrap interval for the gain over the best baseline includes zero (MAE gain $+0.0045$, 95\\% CI $[-0.0052, +0.0134]$), so we do not claim an improvement. Error roughly doubles and rank correlation falls from 0.956 to 0.730 for targets stronger than all sources. The study is limited to one budget, binary scores, and no single-factor ablations; the extrapolation gap is the largest measured difference, reported as point estimates without an interval.\n48:\\paragraph{Answer.} Measured on five benchmarks disjoint from those used to develop the configuration, the point estimates favour the stacked configuration on every error metric, but the evidence does not resolve the gain over the best baseline (a plain random-subset mean): the paired bootstrap interval includes zero. The largest measured difference, reported as point estimates without an interval, is a gap between regimes: MAE is 0.0409 for stronger-than-source targets versus 0.0179 for random targets, and Spearman correlation is 0.730 versus 0.956. The same pattern appears on the five development benchmarks. Part of this gap may reflect the narrower score range of strong targets rather than extrapolation as such, which we did not test.\n196:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} The stacked configuration has lower point-estimate MAE than three matched baselines on five held-out benchmarks, passes both negative controls (each a single episode), and shows a large degradation for stronger-than-source targets. It does not establish a statistically resolved improvement over a random-subset mean, a superiority claim, or generalization to other benchmarks, budgets or score types. The evaluator's ``qualified'' label is not a superiority or generalization claim, and no gate-level diagnostics are available to interpret it further.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60a3cece-bbe1-44ba-b5f4-9fd51819b922","uuid":"9d488376-a43e-49e7-8aca-7e9955321ac3","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:53:43.351Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 7,\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_protoco