{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ro22m29YZCmrsRkfgwZsoS","type":"tool_result","content":"38:Evaluating a language model on every item of a benchmark is expensive, so a natural shortcut is to run the model on a small item subset and extrapolate its full-benchmark accuracy, using the per-item results of previously evaluated ``source'' models to choose the subset. We study this setting with binary per-item results, a budget of $k{=}100$ items, and two kinds of new model: targets that res\n48:\\paragraph{Answer.} Measured on five benchmarks disjoint from those used to develop the configuration, the point estimates favour the stacked configuration on every error metric, but the evidence does not resolve the gain over the best baseline (a plain random-subset mean): the paired bootstrap interval includes zero. The largest measured difference, reported as point estimates without an inter\n50:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (1) A precisely specified estimator family that combines a logistic latent-ability model, Fisher D-optimal item design at upper-tail reference abilities, and non-negative stacking validated on source-only pseudo-targets with strongest-holdout folds for the extrapolation regime (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (2) A controlled held-out comparison against three matched basel\n95:\\textbf{Baselines} (evaluator-owned re-implementations, same $k=100$, same episodes): \\emph{random mean}, the target's mean over $k$ random items; \\emph{anchor points}, $k$-means with $k$ clusters on item response columns of the source matrix, medoid anchors weighted by cluster size; and \\emph{IRT-style}, a three-dimensional logistic IRT model fitted by Adam (200 iterations; \\texttt{evaluate\\_f\n107:\\paragraph{Controls and qualification.} Two negative controls replace the target responses on the selected items: a permutation that assigns each target another target's answers, and a constant response pattern equal to the source majority. The final evaluation is labeled \\emph{qualified} by the evaluator, with completed status and 32 of 32 episodes completed. We read this label as a statement\n166:\\subsection{Negative controls}\n196:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} The stacked configuration has lower point-estimate MAE than three matched baselines on five held-out benchmarks, passes both negative controls (each a single episode), and shows a large degradation for stronger-than-source targets. It does not establish a statistically resolved improvement over a random-subset mean, a superiority claim, or generalization\n212:We evaluated a source-validated stacked latent-ability estimator with Fisher D-optimal item design for predicting a new model's full-benchmark accuracy from 100 items. On five held-out benchmarks it had the lowest point-estimate MAE among the compared estimators (0.0294 versus 0.0339 for the random-subset mean) and passed both negative controls, but the interval for its gain over the best base\n/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/generate-concept-figure/SKILL.md:7:During **paper generation the orchestrator owns this pipeline**: `src/scripts/paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` run the installed `autoillustrate` CLI synchronously as a post-draft step (`run_concept_figure_step`), derive every argument from `paper_strategy.json`, forward the selected outer agent, and promote the result to `./figures/<figure_id>.png`. The phase agents always run in a dedicated one-shot Docker container, even when the outer paper workflow is native; this limits them to the paper directory, selected-agent auth, and selected provider credentials. The paper-writing agent must **not** invoke this pipeline itself. The rest of this document describes the same CLI for standalone/manual use.\n/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/generate-concept-figure/SKILL.md:49:4. Run the pipeline against the manuscript. Resolve the strategy's exact figure id, pass `--output ./figures/<figure_id>.png`, forward the selected `--agent`, and provide a concise grounded prompt. Add `--skip-enhance` when no enhancement backend is available.\n/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/generate-concept-figure/SKILL.md:51:**This pipeline can take ~1h** (it dispatches several selected-agent jobs and may run a VLM restyle). Run it **synchronously and block on it** — the caller must own the pipeline's full lifetime. Do **not** launch it as a background task that outlives the caller's turn (`run_in_background: true`, a trailing `&`, or a fire-and-forget agent step): when that turn ends the pipeline is orphaned and reaped mid-run, leaving the figure unrendered. In `paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` this is exactly what `run_concept_figure_step` does — a single synchronous invocation in the agent container, wrapped with `timeout` or Homebrew's `gtimeout` so a genuine hang is bounded. Native systems without either command skip this non-gating render step instead of launching an unbounded process. The pipeline writes `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (and `<figure_id>.prompts.json`) only on success; confirm those files exist before treating the figure as done.\ngenerate-concept-figure\nmemory-log\nretrieve-references\nscientific-evidence\nvalidate-paper\nwrite-paper-standalone\n11 {'claim': 'Negative controls: permuted Spearman drop 1.157, MAE increase 0.156; constant MAE increase 0.094', 'source_ids': ['final.metric.control_permuted_spearman_drop', 'final.metric.control_permuted_mae_increase', 'final.metric.control_constant_mae_delta'], 'citation_keys': []}\n16 {'claim': 'Search-table metrics for linear variant (MAE 0.0285, strong Spearman 0.771, controls) and extension', 'source_ids': ['result.top_program_03', 'result.top_program_02'], 'citation_keys': []}\n23 {'claim': 'Implementation and protocol details (hyperparameters, folds, designs, baselines, controls matched to one episode on one benchmark and seed, bootstrap settings, software pins) are read from readable sources in ./source_evidence/ (candidate_program.py, extension_program.py