{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PaSSeeepZ8enSPE2WMr1Tb","type":"tool_result","content":"\\paragraph{Controls and qualification.} Two negative controls replace the target responses on the selected items: a permutation that assigns each target another target's answers, and a constant response pattern equal to the source majority. The final evaluation is labeled \\emph{qualified} by the evaluator, with completed status and 32 of 32 episodes completed. We read this label as a statement about the evaluation outcome only, not as evidence of superiority or generalization. The evidence files mark the candidate-side diagnostics as unverified and provide no gate-level diagnostics, so we do not state which criteria underlie the label and attribute nothing to any individual criterion.\n\\subsection{Negative controls}\nPermuting target answers across targets raises MAE by 0.156 and lowers Spearman by 1.157 relative to the matched real episode, and replacing answers by a constant pattern raises MAE by 0.094. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.control_constant_mae_delta]]\nPredictions therefore depend on the target's revealed answers, as one would require of a valid estimator. The controls do not measure accuracy. Each control is a single episode matched to one real random-target episode, on one benchmark (MMLU-Pro) and the first seed of the final evaluation (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}, episode plan), so the control deltas carry no benchmark or seed variance.\n\n\\subsection{Within-family variants on development benchmarks}\nTable~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three configurations of the family on the development benchmarks (two seeds each; all three were scored, none failed). The utility is 0.9381 for the linear model-assisted estimator, 0.9452 for the selected stack and 0.9445 for the stack with bagged posterior means and tail calibration. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c02.combined_score]]\nThe difference between the latter two (0.0006) is smaller than the seed-to-seed spread inside a variant (for the selected stack, 0.9423 versus 0.9480), so the choice of the selected stack over its extension is a frozen choice, not a demonstrated superiority. The extension, which targets strong-target error, did not improve it (strong-target MAE 0.0362 versus 0.0359; Spearman 0.812 versus 0.816), which is consistent with the interpretation that additional tail correction fitted on source pseudo-targets adds nothing measurable here. The gain from the linear estimator to the stack (+0.0071 utility) is larger than the spread but arises from three simultaneous changes (logistic model, Fisher design, stacking) and two seeds, so it cannot be attributed to any component.\nWe evaluated a source-validated stacked latent-ability estimator with Fisher D-optimal item design for predicting a new model's full-benchmark accuracy from 100 items. On five held-out benchmarks it had the lowest point-estimate MAE among the compared estimators (0.0294 versus 0.0339 for the random-subset mean) and passed both negative controls, but the interval for its gain over the best baseline spans zero, so the improvement is not statistically established. The largest measured difference is that targets stronger than every source model are predicted about twice as badly (MAE 0.0409, Spearman 0.730) as random targets (MAE 0.0179, Spearman 0.956), on both development and held-out benchmark groups (point estimates, without intervals, and possibly partly due to the narrower score range of strong targets), and the design aimed at upper-tail abilities did not close this gap. Practically, a user who needs to rank models stronger than the historical pool should not rely on source-matrix-based subset prediction without direct validation on a few full evaluations. Future work should test a budget sweep, single-factor ablations and per-regime baseline comparisons to learn which component, if any, helps, and whether item selection can be made robust for out-of-range targets.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60a3cece-bbe1-44ba-b5f4-9fd51819b922","uuid":"e30cca9e-53e9-4c15-9569-4cc4f322496b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:53:49.154Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\paragraph{Controls and qualification.} Two negative controls replace the target responses on the selected items: a permutation that assigns each target another target's answers, and a constant response pattern equal to the source majority. The final evaluation is labeled \\emph{qualified} by the evaluator, with completed status and 32 of 32 episodes completed. We read this label as a statement about the evaluation outcome only, not as evidence of superiority or generalization. The evidence files mark the candidate-side diagnostics as unverified and provide no gate-level diagnostics, so we do not state which criteria underlie the label and attribute nothing to any individual criterion.\n\\subsection{Negative controls}\nPermuting target answers across targets raises MAE by 0.156 and lowers Spearman by 1.157 relative to the matched real episode, and replacing answers by a constant pattern raises MAE by 0.094. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.control_constant_mae_delta]]\nPredictions therefore depend on the target's revealed answers, as one would require of a valid estimator. The controls do not measure accuracy. Each control is a single episode matched to one real random-target episode, on one benchmark (MMLU-Pro) and the first seed of the final evaluation (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}, episode plan), so the control deltas carry no benchmark or seed variance.\n\n\\subsection{Within-family variants on development benchmarks}\nTable~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three configurations of the family on the development benchmarks (two seeds each; all three were scored, none failed). The utility is 0.9381 for the linear model-assisted estimator, 0.9452 for the selected stack and 0.9445 for the stack with bagged posterior means and tail calibration. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c02.combined_score]]\nThe difference between the latter two (0.0006) is smaller than the seed-to-seed spread inside a variant (for the selected stack, 0.9423 versus 0.9480), so the choice of the selected stack over its extension is a frozen choice, not a demonstrated superiority. The extension, which targets strong-target error, did not improve it (strong-target MAE 0.0362 versus 0.0359; Spearman 0.812 versus 0.816), which is consistent with the interpretation that additional tail correction fitted on source pseudo-targets adds nothing measurable here. The gain from the linear estimator to the stack (+0.0071 utility) is larger than the spread but arises from three simultaneous changes (logistic model, Fisher design, stacking) and two seeds, so it cannot be attributed to any component.\nWe evaluated a source-validated stacked latent-ability estimator with Fisher D-optimal item design for predicting a new model's full-benchmark accuracy from 100 items. On five held-out benchmarks it had the lowest point-estimate MAE among the compared estimators (0.0294 versus 0.0339 for the random-subset mean) and passed both negative controls, but the interval for its gain over the best baseline spans zero, so the improvement is not statistically established. The largest measured difference is that targets stronger than every source model are predicted about twice as badly (MAE 0.0409, Spearman 0.730) as random targets (MAE 0.0179, Spearman 0.956), on both development and held-out benchmark groups (point estimates, without intervals, and possibly partly due to the narrower score range of strong targets), and the design aimed at upper-tail abilities did not close this gap. Practically, a user who needs to rank models stronger than the historical pool should not rely on source-matrix-based subset prediction without direct validation on a few full evaluations. Future work should test a budget sweep, single-factor ablations and per-regime baseline comparisons to learn which component, if any, helps, and whether item selection can be made robust for out-of-range targets.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
